WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.500
An animal's body temperature comes down to a balance.

2
00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:12.280
Heat made by its own metabolism, plus heat gained from the sun, a rock, air or

3
00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:14.700
water, against heat lost to its surroundings.

4
00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:17.840
Animals do not just accept that balance.

5
00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:22.880
They sense temperature, and a controller adjusts how much heat they make, how much

6
00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:24.240
they lose, and what they do.

7
00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:29.320
That is how a desert iguana basking on a rock can end up warmer than you are.

8
00:00:30.283 --> 00:00:31.863
Heat moves by four routes.

9
00:00:33.043 --> 00:00:35.223
Radiation, as when a lizard absorbs sunlight.

10
00:00:36.243 --> 00:00:40.143
Conduction, through direct contact, as from a warm rock into its belly.

11
00:00:41.143 --> 00:00:42.963
Convection, by moving air or water.

12
00:00:43.883 --> 00:00:46.283
And evaporation, as water turns to vapour.

13
00:00:47.123 --> 00:00:48.663
The first three run either way.

14
00:00:49.543 --> 00:00:52.443
Evaporation only removes heat, and it costs water.

15
00:00:53.703 --> 00:00:57.123
Animals adjust these routes with fur and feathers, blood flow to the skin,

16
00:00:57.123 --> 00:01:00.903
countercurrent exchange, sweating or panting, and behaviour.

17
00:01:02.033 --> 00:01:03.193
What adjusts them?

18
00:01:04.033 --> 00:01:08.733
In mammals, sensors in the skin report the air around the body, and sensors in the

19
00:01:08.733 --> 00:01:11.593
brain, spinal cord and abdomen report the core.

20
00:01:12.453 --> 00:01:16.553
A controller in the preoptic area of the hypothalamus weighs those signals.

21
00:01:17.493 --> 00:01:21.933
In the cold, it narrows skin blood vessels and calls for shivering and heat from

22
00:01:21.933 --> 00:01:27.593
brown fat. In the warm, it widens skin vessels and turns on sweating or panting.

23
00:01:28.413 --> 00:01:31.393
Skin sensors can start this before the core has cooled at all.

24
00:01:32.333 --> 00:01:34.773
Where the heat comes from is a separate question.

25
00:01:35.753 --> 00:01:39.773
Mammals and birds are endotherms, warmed mainly by their own metabolism.

26
00:01:40.873 --> 00:01:45.313
Ectotherms, like the desert iguana, take their heat mainly from outside, and steer

27
00:01:45.313 --> 00:01:47.193
it by moving between sun and shade.

28
00:01:48.213 --> 00:01:50.753
Ectotherm names the heat source, not a cold animal.

29
00:01:51.767 --> 00:01:53.467
Keeping warm costs energy.

30
00:01:54.507 --> 00:01:58.147
Plot an endotherm's resting metabolic rate against air temperature.

31
00:01:59.007 --> 00:02:03.767
In the textbook plot, the line is flat inside the thermoneutral zone, at basal

32
00:02:03.767 --> 00:02:09.347
metabolic rate. Below the lower critical temperature, it climbs, as the body makes

33
00:02:09.347 --> 00:02:11.067
more heat to match faster loss.

34
00:02:11.887 --> 00:02:16.307
The steepness of that climb tracks thermal conductance, how readily heat leaks out.

35
00:02:16.307 --> 00:02:21.707
So thick-furred arctic mammals stay flat into much colder air than tropical ones.

36
00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:25.530
The controller can also move the temperature it defends.

37
00:02:26.490 --> 00:02:31.170
In fever, that value is raised, and the body saves heat and makes more to reach it.

38
00:02:31.990 --> 00:02:36.750
In torpor, a bird or mammal lowers its temperature and metabolism under control.

39
00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:42.970
In hibernating black bears, metabolism fell to about a quarter of basal while body

40
00:02:42.970 --> 00:02:48.930
temperature stayed at roughly 30 to 36 degrees C. Cooling alone does not explain

41
00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:50.770
that. Keep one rule.

42
00:02:51.490 --> 00:02:56.110
Heat made and gained equals heat lost, and a controller decides where that balance

43
00:02:56.110 --> 00:02:56.450
sits.
